The rank-four vector bundle $E=f_*\Lambda$ has a perfect symmetric pairing $$\label{trace:form}
 E\otimes E\longrightarrow\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^1},\qquad
 (T_1,T_2)\longmapsto
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{C/\mathbb P^1}\!\left(\frac{T_1T_2}{\eta_C}\right).$$ Its splitting type is $(3,0,0,-3)$. Multiplication by $\xi^2$ identifies $\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^1}(3)$ with its positive line subbundle $\mathcal P$. The form on global sections has radical $\xi^2H^0(C,3H)$, and induces a nondegenerate symmetric form $\langle\ ,\ \rangle$ on $$\label{trace:W}
 W=H^0(C,\Lambda)/\xi^2H^0(C,3H),\qquad \dim W=2.$$ For every $x\in\mathbb P^1$, evaluation identifies this quadratic space with $(\mathcal P^\perp/\mathcal P)_x$.*


*Suppose $q\in Z$, and choose local frames $\mathfrak f$ of $H$ and $\tau$ of $\Lambda$, and a parameter $z$ at $q$. Write, using the fixed isomorphism $2\Lambda\simeq K_C+2H$, $$\tau^2=s(z)\,dz\,\mathfrak f^2,
 \qquad \eta_C=\mathcal E(z)\,dz\,\mathfrak f^2.$$ Coefficient evaluation $\ell_q:T\mapsto(T/\tau)(q)$ descends to $W$. Its square in the dual form is $$\label{trace:dual-evaluation}
 \ell_q^2=\frac{\mathcal E(q)}{s(q)}.$$ In particular, it is nonzero and nonisotropic.

For the curve, line bundles, fixed fourth-root isomorphism, and six vectors $w_j\in H^0(\Gamma,\Phi)^*$ of Proposition <a href="#prop:curve-data" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:curve-data">4</a>, one can choose the basis $u$ with unchanged determinant so that $(u_1)_0$ is an unramified fiber of $f$ disjoint from $Z$. Set $r=\xi u$ and define $$v_j^*=\iota^*w_j\in W^*,\qquad V=\sum_{j=1}^6v_j^*.$$ For any mutually disjoint compatible coordinate disks as above there are nonzero constants $c_j$ and a global pair $$P=(P_1,P_2)\in H^0(C,\theta+Z)^{\oplus2}$$ with the following properties. First, $$\label{trace:vector-identities}
 c_jl_j=v_j^*,\qquad
 V^2=90=9\deg L,\qquad (v_j^*)^2+v_j^*\cdot V=1.$$ On disk $j$, using coefficient rows in the indicated frames, put $$\label{trace:local-data}
 m_j=\frac{c_j}{z_j},\qquad
 R_j=m_jr,\qquad
 W_j=R_j(0)\wedge R_j'(0),\qquad
 p_j=P-m_jr'.$$ The pair $P$ has principal part $R_j(0)/z_j$ in the frame $(\ell_j^{\mathrm{fr}})^2$ of $\theta$; the rows $R_j,p_j$ are holomorphic on the disk, and $R_j(0)\ne0$. Moreover, $$\label{trace:residue-identities}
 W_j=(v_j^*)^2,\qquad
 p_j(0)\wedge R_j(0)=1,\qquad
 \sum_{j=1}^6(W_j-1)=-9\deg L.$$ Globally, $$\label{trace:J}
 J=u\wedge P\in H^0(C,\Lambda),\qquad
 \langle[J],[T]\rangle=-V([T])
       \quad\text{for every }T\in H^0(C,\Lambda).$$ Changing $P$ by a holomorphic pair of sections of $\theta$ does not change $[J]$. The pole prescription is consistent under changes of compatible coordinates.

For $D$ as in <a href="#jets:generator" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="jets:generator">[jets:generator]</a> and $V=t^{\ell+1}(AE_t+B\partial_z)$ one has $$\begin{aligned}
=t^{k+\ell+1}\bigl(&((\ell+1-k)bA+hA'-Bb')E_t\notag\\
 &+((\ell+1)bB+hB'-khA-Bh')\partial_z\bigr).
 \label{jets:general-bracket}
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, $$\label{jets:divergence}
 \mathcal L_D\sigma=t^k\bigl((k-4)b+h'\bigr)\sigma.$$ In particular, for $k\ne4$ the choice $b=h'/(4-k)$ preserves $\sigma$ exactly. The first effect of an order-$k$ modification on <a href="#jets:fields" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="jets:fields">[jets:fields]</a> is $$\label{jets:leading-action}
 \Delta A_k=(1-k)br+hr',\qquad \Delta B_k=-khr.$$

The construction also determines its canonical discrepancy and a lower bound for its geometric genus. The two-form $\omega^\circ$ dual to the frame $w_1,w_2$ generates the canonical sheaf on $X^\circ$. Extending reflexively across the normal surface point gives $\omega_X\simeq\mathcal O_X$; equivalently, we may choose the canonical divisor $K_X=0$. Since a normal surface is Cohen–Macaulay, this reflexive canonical sheaf is its dualizing sheaf, so $X$ is also Gorenstein; this surface criterion is recalled in [[FlennerZaidenberg2001](#ref-FlennerZaidenberg2001), 2.4, Definition 2.18 and the following paragraph]. By <a href="#analytic:ordinary-determinant" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="analytic:ordinary-determinant">[analytic:ordinary-determinant]</a>, its pullback is $$\pi^*\omega^\circ=t^{-5}u(t,z)^{-1}\,dt\wedge dz.$$ It is meromorphic on $Y$ with divisor exactly $-5C$. Thus $$K_Y=\pi^*K_X-5C,$$ so the discrepancy of $C$ is $-5$.

For the geometric genus, put $\mathcal O_{nC}=\mathcal O_Y/\mathcal I^n$. Riemann–Roch and the established equality $h^0(C,L)=2$ give $$h^1(C,L)=h^0(C,L^3)=12.$$ In the sequence $$0\longrightarrow L\longrightarrow\mathcal O_{2C}
 \longrightarrow\mathcal O_C\longrightarrow0,$$ the constant section of $\mathcal O_C$ lifts to $\mathcal O_{2C}$. Since $H^2(C,L)=0$, its cohomology sequence gives $$h^1(\mathcal O_{2C})=h^1(C,L)+h^1(C,\mathcal O_C)=12+21=33.$$ For every $n\geq1$, the sequence with kernel $L^n$ similarly shows that $H^1(\mathcal O_{(n+1)C})\to H^1(\mathcal O_{nC})$ is surjective. The theorem on formal functions, with the cofinal filtrations in <a href="#analytic:cofinal" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="analytic:cofinal">[analytic:cofinal]</a>, identifies their inverse limit with the completion of $(R^1\pi_*\mathcal O_Y)_x$. This stalk has finite length, since the coherent higher direct image is supported at $x$, and thus equals its completion. The inverse limit maps onto $H^1(\mathcal O_{2C})$, proving $$p_g(X,x)=\dim_\mathbb C(R^1\pi_*\mathcal O_Y)_x\geq33.$$ The exceptional curve has genus $g=21$ and its dual graph has first Betti number $b=0$. Consequently $p_g-g-b\geq12$.
